// Code generated by lsp/internal/docgen from docs/SEMANTICS.md and docs/TERRARIUM.md. DO NOT EDIT.

package lsp

// builtinDocs are the builtins, the libraries' members and the Terrarium's
// functions, by name ("len", "math.sin", "fs.read"); "*.name" is a
// drawing target function, which every module that has name shares.
var builtinDocs = map[string]builtinDoc{
	"*.blit":             {Signatures: []string{"blit(target, source, pos, options = {})"}, Doc: "Paints the pixels of `source`, an image or a window, over `target` with its top left at `pos`. It is how a picture gets into a window, and how a window's pixels get into an image (to save a screenshot).\n\nThe options paint it at another size: `\"scale\"`, a number above 0 (`2` is twice as big, `0.25` a quarter), and `\"filter\"`, `\"nearest\"` (the default: each pixel becomes a sharp square, the look of pixel art) or `\"linear\"` (smooth: it mixes the four nearest pixels when enlarging, and averages every pixel it covers when shrinking, so a thumbnail keeps its thin lines). The size is rounded to whole pixels, at least one; colors mix with their alpha.\n```lizard\nwindow.blit(w, img.load(\"lizard.png\"), (10, 10))\nlet shot = img.new(600, 400)\nimg.blit(shot, w, (0, 0))\nimg.save(shot, \"shot.png\")\n\nlet thumb = img.new(150, 100)\nimg.blit(thumb, shot, (0, 0), {\"scale\": 0.25, \"filter\": \"linear\"})\nwindow.blit(w, sprite, (0, 0), {\"scale\": 4})   -~> big, sharp pixels\n```"},
	"*.clear":            {Signatures: []string{"clear(target, color, rect = all)"}, Doc: "Every pixel becomes `color`, or every pixel of the rectangle `rect`. A transparent color erases.\n```lizard\nwindow.clear(w, #f4efe6)\nimg.clear(layer, #00000000, [10, 10, 40, 20])\n```"},
	"*.clip":             {Signatures: []string{"clip(target, rect)"}, Doc: "`clip` cuts every drawing on the target to the rectangle `rect` until `unclip`; a new clip replaces the old one. `clear` keeps to the clip too. A panel that draws its contents with no care for its edges stays inside them:\n```lizard\nwindow.clip(w, [20, 40, 200, 120])\ndraw_list(items)\nwindow.unclip(w)\n```"},
	"*.composite":        {Signatures: []string{"composite(target, source, mode = \"normal\", opacity = 1, pos = (0, 0))"}, Doc: "Mixes `source`, any target, onto `target` with its top left at `pos`, with a blend mode and an opacity: `blit` with a blend mode. A layer made once in an image can be composited many times.\n```lizard\nimg.composite(w, glow, \"screen\", 0.6, (40, 40))\n```"},
	"*.fill":             {Signatures: []string{"fill(target, rect, color)"}, Doc: "Paints the rectangle `rect`, an array `[x, y, w, h]` of numbers.\n```lizard\nimg.fill(picture, [20, 54, 215.5, 20], #ffffffc0)\n```"},
	"*.pop_layer":        {Signatures: []string{"pop_layer(target, mode = \"normal\", opacity = 1)"}, Doc: "`push_layer` opens a new transparent layer on the target, with the clip it had: everything drawn on the target goes to the layer until `pop_layer`, which closes it and mixes it onto what is below with a blend mode and an opacity (0 to 1). Layers nest, up to 64. A group of shapes drawn at half opacity this way doesn't show its own overlaps, and a group in `\"multiply\"` darkens what is below like ink:\n```lizard\nwindow.push_layer(w)\nwindow.spans(w, leaf, #30d050)\nwindow.spans(w, shadow, #000000)\nwindow.pop_layer(w, \"multiply\", 0.8)\n```\n`pop_layer` with no layer open is an error: `no layer to pop: call push_layer first`."},
	"*.push_layer":       {Signatures: []string{"push_layer(target)"}, Doc: "`push_layer` opens a new transparent layer on the target, with the clip it had: everything drawn on the target goes to the layer until `pop_layer`, which closes it and mixes it onto what is below with a blend mode and an opacity (0 to 1). Layers nest, up to 64. A group of shapes drawn at half opacity this way doesn't show its own overlaps, and a group in `\"multiply\"` darkens what is below like ink:\n```lizard\nwindow.push_layer(w)\nwindow.spans(w, leaf, #30d050)\nwindow.spans(w, shadow, #000000)\nwindow.pop_layer(w, \"multiply\", 0.8)\n```\n`pop_layer` with no layer open is an error: `no layer to pop: call push_layer first`."},
	"*.size":             {Signatures: []string{"size(target)"}, Doc: "`(width, height)` in pixels."},
	"*.span":             {Signatures: []string{"span(target, y, x0, x1, color)"}, Doc: "Paints row `y` (rounded down) from `x0` up to `x1`. A shape is drawn as one span per row:\n```lizard\nfunction circle(target, c, r, color) {\n  for (let dy = -r; dy <= r; dy += 1) {\n    let half = math.sqrt(r * r - dy * dy)\n    img.span(target, c.y + dy, c.x - half, c.x + half, color)\n  }\n}\n```"},
	"*.span_gradient":    {Signatures: []string{"span_gradient(target, y, x0, x1, gradient)"}, Doc: "`span` and `spans` with a gradient instead of a color: each pixel takes the gradient's color at its middle. With `spans_gradient`, the gradient is in the space of `list`, before `offset`, so a shape moved with an offset takes its gradient with it. A gradient is a hash:\n\n| key | what it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `kind` | `\"linear\"` or `\"radial\"` |\n| `origin` | a point: where a linear gradient's `t` is 0, the center of a radial one |\n| `axis` | linear: the direction from `t` 0 to `t` 1 divided by its squared length, so `t = (p - origin) · axis` |\n| `scale` | radial: 1 / the radius, so `t` is the distance from `origin` times `scale` |\n| `stops` | `[[t, color], ...]` in order of `t`, from 0 to 1; two stops at the same `t` make a hard edge |\n| `steps` | optional: 2 or more cuts the gradient into that many flat bands |\n\nBefore the first stop the color is the first stop's, after the last the last one's; between two stops it is mixed, alpha included.\n```lizard\n-~> From (0, 0) to (200, 0): the axis is (200, 0) / 200².\nlet sunset = {\"kind\": \"linear\", \"origin\": (0, 0), \"axis\": (1 / 200.0, 0),\n  \"stops\": [[0, #f6c453], [0.5, #e2557b], [1, #3b4cc0]]}\nwindow.spans_gradient(w, rows, sunset)\nlet sun = {\"kind\": \"radial\", \"origin\": (100, 100), \"scale\": 1 / 40.0, \"stops\": [[0, #ffe9a8], [1, #e2557b]], \"steps\": 4}\nwindow.span_gradient(w, 100, 60, 140, sun)\n```\nMalformed gradients are errors that say what is wrong: `bad gradient: a linear gradient needs a point \"axis\"`."},
	"*.spans":            {Signatures: []string{"spans(target, list, color, offset = (0, 0))"}, Doc: "Paints every `[y, x0, x1]` of `list` in one color, moved by `offset`: the fast way to fill a shape worked out row by row, and to draw it again somewhere else without making a new list.\n```lizard\nwindow.spans(w, [[10, 5, 20], [11, 4, 21], [12, 5, 20]], #2b3a55)\nwindow.spans(w, star, #f6c453, (100, 40))\n```"},
	"*.spans_gradient":   {Signatures: []string{"spans_gradient(target, list, gradient, offset = (0, 0))"}, Doc: "`span` and `spans` with a gradient instead of a color: each pixel takes the gradient's color at its middle. With `spans_gradient`, the gradient is in the space of `list`, before `offset`, so a shape moved with an offset takes its gradient with it. A gradient is a hash:\n\n| key | what it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `kind` | `\"linear\"` or `\"radial\"` |\n| `origin` | a point: where a linear gradient's `t` is 0, the center of a radial one |\n| `axis` | linear: the direction from `t` 0 to `t` 1 divided by its squared length, so `t = (p - origin) · axis` |\n| `scale` | radial: 1 / the radius, so `t` is the distance from `origin` times `scale` |\n| `stops` | `[[t, color], ...]` in order of `t`, from 0 to 1; two stops at the same `t` make a hard edge |\n| `steps` | optional: 2 or more cuts the gradient into that many flat bands |\n\nBefore the first stop the color is the first stop's, after the last the last one's; between two stops it is mixed, alpha included.\n```lizard\n-~> From (0, 0) to (200, 0): the axis is (200, 0) / 200².\nlet sunset = {\"kind\": \"linear\", \"origin\": (0, 0), \"axis\": (1 / 200.0, 0),\n  \"stops\": [[0, #f6c453], [0.5, #e2557b], [1, #3b4cc0]]}\nwindow.spans_gradient(w, rows, sunset)\nlet sun = {\"kind\": \"radial\", \"origin\": (100, 100), \"scale\": 1 / 40.0, \"stops\": [[0, #ffe9a8], [1, #e2557b]], \"steps\": 4}\nwindow.span_gradient(w, 100, 60, 140, sun)\n```\nMalformed gradients are errors that say what is wrong: `bad gradient: a linear gradient needs a point \"axis\"`."},
	"*.triangles":        {Signatures: []string{"triangles(target, list, options = {})"}, Doc: "Fills every triangle of `list`, keeping the nearest pixel: the one step of 3D drawing done pixel by pixel (the [lily3d](flower/LILY3D.md) library does everything else in lizard-script). A triangle is `[a, b, c, color]` in one color, or `[a, color_a, b, color_b, c, color_c]` with the colors mixed from its corners. A corner is a point `(x, y, z)`: `x` and `y` in pixels, `z` its depth, smaller nearer (`(x, y)` is depth 0). A pixel is filled when its middle is inside the triangle, or on a top or left edge, so two triangles that share an edge never both fill a pixel of it, or both miss one. Its depth and color are mixed from the corners' by how near the middle is to each.\n\nThe target keeps a depth buffer: a pixel is only painted when it is nearer than what was painted there before, and then it keeps its depth. Colors with alpha are painted over what is there. Either turn of the corners is drawn (leaving out the backs is the program's job), and what is outside the target or its clip is cut. The options:\n\n| Option | Default | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `depth` | `true` | `false` paints in order, with no depth test |\n| `clear_depth` | `false` | `true` makes the depth buffer empty first: call it once per frame, before the first triangles |\n\n```lizard\nimg.triangles(picture, [[(10, 10, 0.5), (90, 20, 0.5), (40, 80, 0.5), #f2a65a]], {\"clear_depth\": true})\nimg.triangles(picture, [[(0, 0, 0.2), #ff0000, (100, 0, 0.9), #00ff00, (0, 100, 0.9), #0000ff]])\n```\nA malformed triangle is the error `triangle 0 must be [a, b, c, color] or [a, color, b, color, c, color] with points (x, y, z), got ...`. In Go it is `pixels.Image.Triangle` and `ClearDepth` (`internal/pixels/triangles.go`)."},
	"*.unclip":           {Signatures: []string{"unclip(target)"}, Doc: "`clip` cuts every drawing on the target to the rectangle `rect` until `unclip`; a new clip replaces the old one. `clear` keeps to the clip too. A panel that draws its contents with no care for its edges stays inside them:\n```lizard\nwindow.clip(w, [20, 40, 200, 120])\ndraw_list(items)\nwindow.unclip(w)\n```"},
	"clip.get":           {Signatures: []string{"clip.get()"}, Doc: "The text on the clipboard, `\"\"` when there is none."},
	"clip.set":           {Signatures: []string{"clip.set(text)"}, Doc: "Puts `text` on the clipboard.\n```lizard\nclip.set(json.write(drawing))\n```"},
	"delete":             {Signatures: []string{"delete(hash, key)"}, Doc: "Removes `key`, and returns whether it was there."},
	"error":              {Signatures: []string{"error(message)", "error(message, options)"}, Doc: "Stops the program with the runtime error `[line L, column C] runtime error: message`, positioned at the call. A string is used as it is; any other value is turned into text like `str` does.\n\nThe same, with a hash of options. `{\"at\": \"caller\"}` reports the error where the program called into this file instead (see Errors at the caller); `{\"at\": \"here\"}` is the default. Any other key or value is an error of `error` itself. A second argument that is not a hash is still `wrong number of arguments: want 1, got 2`."},
	"first":              {Signatures: []string{"first(list)"}, Doc: "The first or last element, or `null` if `list` is empty."},
	"float":              {Signatures: []string{"float(x)"}, Doc: "An integer, a float or a string with a number (`\"2\"`, `\"-0.25\"`) as a float."},
	"fs.append":          {Signatures: []string{"fs.append(path, text)"}, Doc: "Adds `text` at the end of the file, or makes it.\n```lizard\nfs.append(\"log.txt\", \"one more line\\n\")\n```"},
	"fs.copy":            {Signatures: []string{"fs.copy(from, to)"}, Doc: "Copies a file (not a folder), with its permissions. Like `fs.rename`, it refuses to replace something at `to`.\n```lizard\nfs.copy(\"settings.json\", \"settings.backup.json\")\n```"},
	"fs.digest":          {Signatures: []string{"fs.digest(path, algorithm = \"sha256\")"}, Doc: "The hash of the file's bytes as lowercase hex, like [`text.digest`](SEMANTICS.md#the-text-library) of its text: `\"sha256\"` (64 digits) or `\"fnv\"` (16 digits, fast, for caches). Two files with the same digest have the same contents, so it names a thumbnail or a cache entry after what is in the file. It reads a built program's own files (`bundle:`) too.\n```lizard\nlet name = fs.digest(\"drawing.lzdraw\") + \".png\"\nfs.digest(\"notes.txt\", \"fnv\")   -~> \"e71fa2190541574b\" for a file that holds abc\n```"},
	"fs.exists":          {Signatures: []string{"fs.exists(path)"}, Doc: "Whether there is a file or folder at `path`.\n```lizard\nif (!fs.exists(\"out\")) { fs.mkdir(\"out\") }\n```"},
	"fs.home":            {Signatures: []string{"fs.home()"}, Doc: "The user's home folder.\n```lizard\nlet dir = fs.join(fs.home(), \"Pictures\")\n```"},
	"fs.info":            {Signatures: []string{"fs.info(path)"}, Doc: "What is at `path`: `{\"size\": bytes, \"mtime\": seconds since 1970 when it last changed, \"dir\": whether it is a folder}`. Errors when there is nothing: `can't read x: no such file or directory`.\n```lizard\nlet newer = fs.info(\"a.txt\").mtime > fs.info(\"b.txt\").mtime\n```"},
	"fs.join":            {Signatures: []string{"fs.join(a, b, ...)"}, Doc: "Puts the parts of a path together with `/` and tidies it. Takes one or more strings.\n```lizard\nfs.join(\"a/\", \"./b\", \"../c\")   -~> \"a/c\"\n```"},
	"fs.list":            {Signatures: []string{"fs.list(dir, options = {})"}, Doc: "The names of what is in the folder, files and folders, in alphabetical order. Errors when it isn't a folder. With the option `{\"info\": true}` each one is a hash instead: its `name` and what `fs.info` gives. An unknown option is an error.\n```lizard\nputs(fs.list(\".\"))   -~> [\"README.md\", \"docs\", \"examples\", ...]\nlet files = fs.list(\"drawings\", {\"info\": true})\nputs(files[0])   -~> {\"name\": \"cat.lzdraw\", \"size\": 812, \"mtime\": 1793735180, \"dir\": false}\n```"},
	"fs.mkdir":           {Signatures: []string{"fs.mkdir(path)"}, Doc: "Makes the folder and the ones above it that are missing. A folder that is already there is fine.\n```lizard\nfs.mkdir(\"out/pictures/2026\")\n```"},
	"fs.read":            {Signatures: []string{"fs.read(path)"}, Doc: "The text of the file. Errors when it can't be read: `can't read notes.txt: no such file or directory`.\n```lizard\nlet text = fs.read(\"notes.txt\")\n```"},
	"fs.remove":          {Signatures: []string{"fs.remove(path)"}, Doc: "Deletes a file or an empty folder. It refuses a folder with something in it (`can't remove out: the folder is not empty`), so a mistake can't delete a whole tree.\n```lizard\nfs.remove(\"notes.txt\")\n```"},
	"fs.rename":          {Signatures: []string{"fs.rename(from, to)"}, Doc: "Moves a file or folder to a new path (also to another folder). It refuses to replace something that is already at `to`: `can't move a.txt to b.txt: there is already something there`, so a mistake can't lose a file.\n```lizard\nfs.rename(\"draft.txt\", \"final.txt\")\n```"},
	"fs.trash":           {Signatures: []string{"fs.trash(path)"}, Doc: "Moves a file or folder to the Trash, where the user can get it back: `~/.Trash` on macOS (Finder, when the file is on another disk), the Recycle Bin on Windows, the freedesktop Trash on Linux (`$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash`, with the `.trashinfo` file managers use to put it back; `gio trash` for another disk). A name already in the Trash gets ` 2`, ` 3`, ... before its extension. It never deletes: when the Trash can't take it, the file stays and it is the error `can't move x to the Trash: ...`.\n```lizard\nfs.trash(\"old drawing.lzdraw\")\n```"},
	"fs.write":           {Signatures: []string{"fs.write(path, text)"}, Doc: "Replaces the file with `text`, or makes it. Another program never sees half a file: the text goes to a new file next to it, which then takes its place. A file that was there keeps its permissions. Errors when the folder doesn't exist or `path` is a folder.\n```lizard\nfs.write(\"notes.txt\", \"lizards like sun\\n\")\n```"},
	"hash.copy":          {Signatures: []string{"hash.copy(h)"}, Doc: "A new hash with the same keys and values, in the same order. The values themselves are shared, like `list.copy`."},
	"hash.has":           {Signatures: []string{"hash.has(h, key)"}, Doc: "Whether `key` is in `h`, also when its value is `null` (both read as `null` with `h[key]`)."},
	"hash.keys":          {Signatures: []string{"hash.keys(h)"}, Doc: "The same arrays as `keys(h)` and `values(h)`."},
	"hash.merge":         {Signatures: []string{"hash.merge(a, b)"}, Doc: "A new hash with the keys of `a` in their order, then the keys of `b` that `a` doesn't have, in theirs. Where both have a key, the value is `b`'s."},
	"hash.remove":        {Signatures: []string{"hash.remove(h, key)"}, Doc: "Removes `key`, and returns whether it was there, like `delete`."},
	"hash.values":        {Signatures: []string{"hash.values(h)"}, Doc: "The same arrays as `keys(h)` and `values(h)`."},
	"hsl":                {Signatures: []string{"hsl(h, s, l)"}, Doc: "A color from a hue in degrees (0 to 360, so `360` is the same as `0`) and a saturation and lightness in percent (0 to 100). Anything outside is an error. Channels are rounded: `hsl(210, 50, 40)` is `#336699`."},
	"img.from_pixels":    {Signatures: []string{"img.from_pixels(size, pixels)"}, Doc: "A new image of `size`, a point `(w, h)`, made from a flat list of `w * h` colors in the order `img.pixels` gives them.\n```lizard\nlet flipped = img.from_pixels(img.size(picture), list.reverse(img.pixels(picture)))\n```"},
	"img.get":            {Signatures: []string{"img.get(image, x, y)"}, Doc: "The color of the pixel at column `x`, row `y`, from the top left. Errors outside the image: `the pixel (2, 0) is outside the 2x2 image`."},
	"img.load":           {Signatures: []string{"img.load(path, max_side = 0)"}, Doc: "Reads a PNG, JPEG or GIF file (a built program's own files too). With a `max_side` above 0, an image longer than that on its longer side is shrunk to fit, averaging the pixels (it is never enlarged). Errors when the file can't be read, isn't an image, or is more than 32768 pixels on a side.\n```lizard\nlet photo = img.load(\"photo.jpg\", 256)\n```"},
	"img.new":            {Signatures: []string{"img.new(w, h, color = #00000000)"}, Doc: "A new image of `w` by `h` pixels (0 to 32768 each, at most 64M pixels in all), filled with `color`.\n```lizard\nlet picture = img.new(256, 128, #ffffff)\n```"},
	"img.pixels":         {Signatures: []string{"img.pixels(image)"}, Doc: "Every color of the image in one flat list, row by row from the top left: the pixel `(x, y)` is at index `y * width + x`. A flat list is the fastest way for a loop to read a whole image."},
	"img.png_base64":     {Signatures: []string{"img.png_base64(target, rect = all)"}, Doc: "The PNG of a target (or of the part `rect` of it) as base64 text: a picture another program can take inside JSON. Errors when `rect` is all outside the target.\n```lizard\nlet data = img.png_base64(window.screenshot(w))\n```"},
	"img.save":           {Signatures: []string{"img.save(target, path, rect = all)"}, Doc: "Writes an image, a window or a surface as a PNG file, the whole file at once like `fs.write`. With `rect`, `[x, y, w, h]`, only that part of it.\n```lizard\nimg.save(picture, \"out.png\")\nimg.save(picture, \"corner.png\", [0, 0, 64, 64])\n```"},
	"img.set":            {Signatures: []string{"img.set(image, x, y, color)"}, Doc: "Replaces the pixel with `color`. It doesn't mix it with the color below; `img.fill` and the other drawing functions do.\n```lizard\nfor (let x = 0; x < 256; x += 1) { img.set(picture, x, 0, rgb(x, 0, 0)) }\n```"},
	"int":                {Signatures: []string{"int(x)"}, Doc: "A string with a whole number, a boolean, an integer or a float as an integer. A float loses its decimals towards zero: `int(-1.9)` is `-1`. `int(\"1.5\")` is an error."},
	"io.error":           {Signatures: []string{"io.error(text)"}, Doc: "Writes `text` to the error output, with nothing after it. A program that talks through its output (a server) logs here.\n```lizard\nio.error(\"got a request\\n\")\n```\n\nIn Go, `terrarium.Stdin` and `terrarium.Stderr` are where they read and write (`repl.RunFile` points `Stderr` at its error writer), and `io.write` writes to `evaluator.Output`, like `puts`."},
	"io.read_line":       {Signatures: []string{"io.read_line()"}, Doc: "The next line of the input, without its line break (and without a `\\r` before it), or `null` when the input has ended. A last line with no line break still counts. Lines longer than 32 MB are an error.\n```lizard\nlet line = io.read_line()\nwhile (line != null) {\n  puts(text.upper(line))\n  line = io.read_line()\n}\n```"},
	"io.write":           {Signatures: []string{"io.write(text)"}, Doc: "Writes `text` where `puts` writes, with nothing after it, so `io.write(\"a\"); puts(\"b\")` prints `ab`."},
	"json.parse":         {Signatures: []string{"json.parse(text)"}, Doc: "The value of the JSON text. A mistake is an error that says where it is: `json.parse: line 2, column 3: unexpected 'x', expected a value`. Escapes, `\\u` escapes and surrogate pairs are decoded. Objects and arrays nest at most 100 deep.\n```lizard\nlet lizard = json.parse(fs.read(\"liz.json\"))\nputs(lizard.name)\n```"},
	"json.write":         {Signatures: []string{"json.write(value, indent = 0)"}, Doc: "The JSON text of `value`. With an `indent` above 0 (at most 16), each item goes on its own line, nested by that many spaces. A `COLOR` is written as its `\"#rrggbb\"` text and a `POINT` as `[x, y]` or `[x, y, z]`; a hash key that isn't a string is written with `str()`. A function, or a value that contains itself, is an error: `json.write: can't write a FUNCTION as JSON`.\n```lizard\nfs.write(\"liz.json\", json.write({\"name\": \"Liz\", \"toes\": [5, 5, 5, 5]}, 2))\n```"},
	"keys":               {Signatures: []string{"keys(hash)"}, Doc: "Arrays with the keys or the values, in the order the keys were added."},
	"last":               {Signatures: []string{"last(list)"}, Doc: "The first or last element, or `null` if `list` is empty."},
	"len":                {Signatures: []string{"len(x)"}, Doc: "The number of characters of a string, or of elements of an array or hash."},
	"list.copy":          {Signatures: []string{"list.copy(a)"}, Doc: "A new array with the same elements (the elements themselves are shared)."},
	"list.index":         {Signatures: []string{"list.index(a, x)"}, Doc: "The index of the first element that is `=== x`, or `-1`."},
	"list.insert":        {Signatures: []string{"list.insert(a, i, x)"}, Doc: "Puts `x` before index `i` (`len(a)` adds at the end), changes `a` in place and returns it, like `push`. An index outside `0` to `len(a)` is an error."},
	"list.remove":        {Signatures: []string{"list.remove(a, i)"}, Doc: "Takes out the element at `i`, changes `a` in place and returns `a`. An index that doesn't exist is an error."},
	"list.reverse":       {Signatures: []string{"list.reverse(a)"}, Doc: "A new array in the opposite order."},
	"list.slice":         {Signatures: []string{"list.slice(a, start, end)"}, Doc: "A new array with the elements from `start` up to `end`, clamped like `text.slice`."},
	"list.sort":          {Signatures: []string{"list.sort(a, [less])"}, Doc: "A new array in order, keeping the order of equal elements. Without `less` the elements must be all numbers (by value) or all strings (by characters). `less(x, y)` is a lizard function with two parameters that says whether `x` goes before `y`. An error inside it stops the sort and is reported with the position inside `less`."},
	"lizard.check":       {Signatures: []string{"lizard.check(code, name = \"\", names = [])"}, Doc: "Finds mistakes in `code` without running it, the way `lizardscript check` does (package `check`): an array with one hash per finding, in the order of the code.\n\n| Key | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `severity` | `\"error\"` (the code fails when it runs, or doesn't run at all) or `\"warning\"` (probably a mistake) |\n| `rule` | which check found it: `\"syntax\"`, `\"lexical\"`, `\"unknown-name\"`, `\"assign\"`, `\"arguments\"`, `\"member\"`, `\"module\"`, `\"return\"` (errors); `\"unused\"`, `\"shadow\"`, `\"unreachable\"`, `\"literals\"`, `\"unused-module\"`, `\"self-assign\"` (warnings). `member` is an error when the member is called (`math.sine(1)`), a warning when it is only read |\n| `message` | what is wrong, without the position |\n| `hint` | how to fix it, like ``\"did you mean `size`?\"``, or `null` |\n| `line`, `column`, `end_line`, `end_column` | what it is about |\n\nThe code has the Terrarium's names (and the builtins and libraries); `names` lists more that the host running it will define, so they aren't unknown. `name` is its source name, where its `use` statements look for modules; a module that can't be found is an error, and then any name may come from it. With syntax errors, only the syntax errors and the rules that need no names are reported.\n\n```lizard\nlet found = lizard.check(\"let size = 10\\nputs(siz * 2)\")\nputs(found[0].severity, found[0].message, found[0].hint)   -~> error identifier not found: siz did you mean `size`?\nputs(lizard.check(\"draw(canvas)\", \"\", [\"draw\", \"canvas\"]))   -~> []\n```"},
	"lizard.docs":        {Signatures: []string{"lizard.docs(code)"}, Doc: "The doc comments of the functions at the top level of `code`: the `-~>` lines right above each `function name(...)` or `let name = function(...)`. It gives a hash from each name to `{\"name\", \"line\", \"summary\", \"params\", \"example\"}`:\n\n- `summary` is the first lines, up to a blank `-~>` line, a parameter line or the example, joined with spaces;\n- `params` has one `{\"name\", \"default\", \"doc\"}` per parameter: the default as written (`\"(1, 2)\"`) or `null`, and the text of its `name: ...` line (lines after it go on it) or `null`;\n- `example` is what follows `example:` (lines after it go on it, with line breaks), or `null`.\n\n```lizard\n-~> A circle around center.\n-~> radius: how big, in pixels\n-~> example: circle((10, 10), 5)\nfunction circle(center, radius = 1) { ... }\n```\ngives `{\"circle\": {\"name\": \"circle\", \"line\": 4, \"summary\": \"A circle around center.\", \"params\": [{\"name\": \"center\", \"default\": null, \"doc\": null}, {\"name\": \"radius\", \"default\": \"1\", \"doc\": \"how big, in pixels\"}], \"example\": \"circle((10, 10), 5)\"}}`. Code with syntax errors gives the functions that parsed."},
	"lizard.parse":       {Signatures: []string{"lizard.parse(code, name = \"\")"}, Doc: "The syntax tree of `code`: `{\"program\": node, \"errors\": [...]}`. Each error is `{\"kind\": \"syntax\" or \"lexical\", \"message\", \"line\", \"column\"}`, the message without its position. With errors, the program has the statements that parsed. `name` is the source name of the code.\n\nEach node is a hash with its `kind`, its positions (a node in parentheses includes them; a statement includes its `;`) and the fields of its kind. A missing part (an `else` that isn't there, a parameter without a default) is `null`.\n\n| kind | fields |\n| --- | --- |\n| `program`, `block` | `statements` |\n| `let` | `name`, `value`, `declaration` (true when written `function name(...) { }`) |\n| `return` | `value` |\n| `break`, `continue` | |\n| `try` | `body`, `error_name` (the name in `catch (e)`), `catch` |\n| `expression` | `expression` (a statement that is an expression) |\n| `layer` | `name` (a string), `body` (a `block` without braces) |\n| `use` | `path` (a `string` node), `as` |\n| `name` | `value` |\n| `integer`, `float`, `string`, `color`, `boolean` | `value` |\n| `point` | `coords` |\n| `prefix` | `operator`, `right` |\n| `infix` | `operator`, `left`, `right` |\n| `assign` | `operator` (`\"=\"`, `\"+=\"`, ...), `target`, `value` |\n| `if` | `condition`, `consequence`, `alternative` (an `elif` is an `if` alone in the alternative) |\n| `while` | `condition`, `body` |\n| `for` | `init`, `condition`, `update`, `body` |\n| `function` | `params` (`name` nodes), `defaults` (one node or `null` per parameter), `body` |\n| `call` | `callee`, `arguments` |\n| `array` | `elements` |\n| `index` | `left`, `index` |\n| `dot` | `left`, `name` (a `string` node): `a.b` |\n| `hash` | `pairs` (each `{\"key\", \"value\"}`) |\n\n```lizard\nlet tree = lizard.parse(\"let area = w * h\")\nlet value = tree.program.statements[0].value\nputs(value.kind, value.operator, value.column, value.end_column)   -~> infix * 12 17\n```"},
	"lizard.run":         {Signatures: []string{"lizard.run(code, options = {})"}, Doc: "Runs `code` in a sandbox: a new top scope with only the builtins, the libraries and what `options.globals` gives (no Terrarium, unless given), from a fresh start (its own step count, the default random seed, new libraries, its own modules). The program that calls it goes on exactly as before: its steps, random numbers, output and hooks are its own again. It gives:\n\n| Key | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `value` | the code's value, `null` when it failed |\n| `error` | `null`, or `{\"kind\": \"syntax\", \"lexical\" or \"runtime\", \"message\", \"line\", \"column\", \"source\"}`, the message without its position (for a syntax error, the first one) |\n| `errors` | every error: each syntax error of a code that doesn't parse, in order (so a host doesn't parse it again to list them), the one that stopped a code that ran, or none; `error` is the first |\n| `output` | what the code printed, with `puts` or `io.write` |\n| `steps` | the steps it took, while a limit was set |\n| `globals` | a hash of its top-level names after the run, the given ones included |\n\nThe options:\n\n| Option | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `globals` | a hash of names to define first, like `{\"fs\": fs, \"size\": 10}` |\n| `steps` | the most steps it may take (a loop round or a call is one), 0 for no limit. Past it, the error is `the program ran too long (more than N steps)` |\n| `name` | its source name, the `source` of its errors, and where its `use` statements look for modules |\n| `engine` | `\"evaluator\"` (the default) or `\"vm\"`; both give the same results |\n| `use` | a list of module paths, like `[\"draw/shapes\", \"draw/colors\"]`: the code sees them alive before its first line, as if it started with `use \"draw/shapes\"` for each one, found from its `name`. Writing a module's variable (`cache_limit = 4`) changes the module itself, which a copy in `globals` can't. They load once per run, on the code's engine; a module that fails is the run's `error` |\n| `on_statement` | a function called with the line, the column and the source name before each statement runs |\n| `hook_source` | a source name: `on_statement` is called only for the statements of that source, usually the code's own `name`, not for the ones of its modules. Much cheaper than leaving the filter to the hook |\n| `hook_functions` | `true` (the default) or `false`: with `false`, `on_statement` is not called for the statements of function bodies, only for the ones outside every function (a module's too, and the ones in layers, blocks and loops). On the VM, the functions then run without the hook's instructions |\n| `on_layer` | a function called with the layer's name, `true` or `false` and its line when a layer starts and ends |\n\nThe two hooks run as the calling program: their steps count in its limit, and what they print is its output. An error in a hook stops the run and is the error of `lizard.run` (`lizard.run: a hook failed: ...`). Modules the code uses see the same `globals` around them. A module's file is parsed (and, on the VM, compiled) once while its text stays the same, so many runs with the same libraries don't pay for them again; their code runs once in each run.\n```lizard\nlet r = lizard.run(\"let area = w * h\\nputs(area)\", {\"globals\": {\"w\": 3, \"h\": 4}, \"steps\": 1000})\nputs(r.output, r.globals.area, r.error)   -~> 12\\n 12 null\n\nlet drawing = lizard.run(code, {\"name\": \"drawing.liz\", \"use\": [\"draw/shapes\"], \"hook_source\": \"drawing.liz\",\n  \"on_statement\": function(line, column, source) { rows = rows + [line] }})\n```"},
	"lizard.tokens":      {Signatures: []string{"lizard.tokens(code, options = {})"}, Doc: "The tokens of `code`, comments included, in order. Each one is a hash:\n\n| Key | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `kind` | for coloring: `\"name\"`, `\"keyword\"`, `\"number\"`, `\"string\"`, `\"color\"`, `\"operator\"`, `\"punctuation\"`, `\"comment\"` or `\"error\"` (something the lexer rejected, like an unterminated string) |\n| `type` | the exact token: `\"IDENT\"`, `\"INT\"`, `\"FLOAT\"`, `\"LET\"`, `\"+=\"`, `\"(\"`, `\"COMMENT\"`, ... |\n| `text` | what is written, quotes and escapes included |\n| `value` | what it means: a string without quotes and escapes, `45deg` in radians |\n| `line`, `column`, `end_line`, `end_column` | where it is |\n\n```lizard\nputs(lizard.tokens(\"x = 45deg\")[2])\n-~> {\"kind\": \"number\", \"type\": \"FLOAT\", \"text\": \"45deg\", \"value\": \"0.7853981633974483\", \"line\": 1, \"column\": 5, \"end_line\": 1, \"end_column\": 10}\n```\n\nWith `{\"columns\": true}` it is one hash of those keys instead, each a list with one element per token: `kind[i]`, `text[i]`, `line[i]` and the rest are token `i`. No hash is made per token, so a program that walks the tokens of a big text (an editor's scanner) gets them about four times faster (220,000 tokens: about 30 ms against 125 ms on an M4); the strings that repeat, like each kind and type and the text of each operator, are one object each. An unknown option is an error.\n```lizard\nlet t = lizard.tokens(\"x = 45deg\", {\"columns\": true})\nputs(t.kind, t.column)   -~> [\"name\", \"operator\", \"number\"] [1, 3, 5]\n```"},
	"math.abs":           {Signatures: []string{"math.abs(x)"}, Doc: "Without the sign. An integer for an integer, a float for a float."},
	"math.acos":          {Signatures: []string{"math.acos(x)"}, Doc: "The angle in radians whose sine or cosine is `x`, as a float. `x` must be from -1 to 1, otherwise it is an error like `sqrt(-1)`."},
	"math.angle":         {Signatures: []string{"math.angle(p, q)"}, Doc: "On points, see Points above."},
	"math.asin":          {Signatures: []string{"math.asin(x)"}, Doc: "The angle in radians whose sine or cosine is `x`, as a float. `x` must be from -1 to 1, otherwise it is an error like `sqrt(-1)`."},
	"math.atan2":         {Signatures: []string{"math.atan2(y, x)"}, Doc: "The angle of the point `(x, y)`, as a float."},
	"math.ceil":          {Signatures: []string{"math.ceil(x)"}, Doc: "An integer. `round` goes away from zero on halves: `math.round(2.5)` is `3`, `math.round(-2.5)` is `-3`. An integer is returned as it is, and a float too big for an integer is an error."},
	"math.cos":           {Signatures: []string{"math.cos(x)"}, Doc: "As a float."},
	"math.dist":          {Signatures: []string{"math.dist(p, q)"}, Doc: "On points, see Points above."},
	"math.floor":         {Signatures: []string{"math.floor(x)"}, Doc: "An integer. `round` goes away from zero on halves: `math.round(2.5)` is `3`, `math.round(-2.5)` is `-3`. An integer is returned as it is, and a float too big for an integer is an error."},
	"math.max":           {Signatures: []string{"math.max(a, b, ...)"}, Doc: "The smallest or largest of at least two numbers, compared by value. The result is that argument itself, so `math.min(1, 2.5)` is the integer `1`; on a tie the first one."},
	"math.min":           {Signatures: []string{"math.min(a, b, ...)"}, Doc: "The smallest or largest of at least two numbers, compared by value. The result is that argument itself, so `math.min(1, 2.5)` is the integer `1`; on a tie the first one."},
	"math.pi":            {Signatures: []string{}, Doc: "π and 2π, as floats."},
	"math.random":        {Signatures: []string{"math.random()", "math.random(a, b)"}, Doc: "A float from 0 up to but not including 1.\n\nAn integer from `a` to `b`, both included. An error if `b < a`."},
	"math.round":         {Signatures: []string{"math.round(x)"}, Doc: "An integer. `round` goes away from zero on halves: `math.round(2.5)` is `3`, `math.round(-2.5)` is `-3`. An integer is returned as it is, and a float too big for an integer is an error."},
	"math.seed":          {Signatures: []string{"math.seed(n)"}, Doc: "Restarts `random` from the integer `n`. Returns `null`."},
	"math.sin":           {Signatures: []string{"math.sin(x)"}, Doc: "As a float."},
	"math.sqrt":          {Signatures: []string{"math.sqrt(x)"}, Doc: "The square root, as a float. A negative `x` is an error."},
	"math.tan":           {Signatures: []string{"math.tan(x)"}, Doc: "As a float."},
	"math.tau":           {Signatures: []string{}, Doc: "π and 2π, as floats."},
	"null":               {Signatures: []string{}, Doc: "The value of nothing: what a function without a value gives. `type(null)` is `\"NULL\"`; it is false in conditions and equal only to `null`."},
	"os.args":            {Signatures: []string{"os.args()"}, Doc: "The program's arguments as strings, the ones after its file: `lizardscript tool.liz in.png out.png` gives `[\"in.png\", \"out.png\"]`.\n```lizard\nlet files = os.args()\n```"},
	"os.config_dir":      {Signatures: []string{"os.config_dir()"}, Doc: "The folder where programs keep the user's settings: `~/Library/Application Support` on macOS, `%AppData%` on Windows, `~/.config` on Linux. It doesn't make it.\n```lizard\nlet settings = fs.join(os.config_dir(), \"my-tool\", \"settings.json\")\n```"},
	"os.env":             {Signatures: []string{"os.env(name)"}, Doc: "The value of the environment variable, or `null` when it isn't set.\n```lizard\nlet editor = os.env(\"EDITOR\")\n```"},
	"os.exit":            {Signatures: []string{"os.exit(code = 0)"}, Doc: "Ends the program now with the exit code, 0 to 255.\n```lizard\nif (len(os.args()) == 0) { puts(\"usage: tool.liz file\"); os.exit(2) }\n```"},
	"os.locale":          {Signatures: []string{"os.locale()"}, Doc: "The user's language as a lowercase two-letter code, like `\"es\"`. It asks the system, then `LC_ALL` and `LANG`, and is `\"en\"` when none of them says."},
	"os.memory":          {Signatures: []string{"os.memory()"}, Doc: "The machine's memory in bytes: `{\"total\": all of it, \"free\": what can be used now}` (free and inactive memory on macOS, `MemAvailable` on Linux). `free` is never more than `total`.\n```lizard\nlet m = os.memory()\nputs(m.free * 100 // m.total, \"% free\")\n```"},
	"os.open_url":        {Signatures: []string{"os.open_url(url)"}, Doc: "Opens a web address in the user's browser, or a `mailto:` address in the mail program. Only `http://`, `https://` and `mailto:` are taken (`only http, https and mailto addresses can be opened`), so a program can't run a file through it.\n```lizard\nos.open_url(\"https://github.com/\")\n```"},
	"os.platform":        {Signatures: []string{"os.platform()"}, Doc: "`\"macos\"`, `\"windows\"` or `\"linux\"`."},
	"os.program_dir":     {Signatures: []string{"os.program_dir()"}, Doc: "The folder of the program's main file, as an absolute path, so a program finds the files that come with it wherever it is run from. In a built program it is `\"bundle:\"`, where `fs` reads its own files. In the REPL it is the current folder.\n```lizard\nlet icons = fs.list(fs.join(os.program_dir(), \"icons\"))\n```"},
	"os.reveal":          {Signatures: []string{"os.reveal(path)"}, Doc: "Shows the file or folder in the file manager: selected in Finder or Explorer, its folder opened on Linux (`xdg-open`). Errors when there is nothing at `path`."},
	"pen.available":      {Signatures: []string{"pen.available()"}, Doc: "Whether this system can report pressure at all: always on macOS, on Windows 8 and later, and on Linux when the X server has XInput 2.1 or later. Even when it is true, a mouse reports 1.\n```lizard\nlet size = 2 + e.pen.pressure * 14\n```"},
	"pen.curve":          {Signatures: []string{"pen.curve(source)", "pen.curve(source, curve)"}, Doc: "The response curve of a source (`\"trackpad\"`, `\"tablet\"` or `\"mouse\"`), as a hash `{\"low\", \"high\", \"gamma\"}`. With a second argument it changes the curve first: the keys left out keep their value, and the call fails with a clear message for an unknown source or key, a value that isn't a number, or one out of range. It returns the curve now in use.\n```lizard\npen.curve(\"trackpad\")                   -~> {\"low\": 0.8, \"high\": 1.0, \"gamma\": 1.5}\npen.curve(\"trackpad\", {\"gamma\": 1.2})   -~> {\"low\": 0.8, \"high\": 1.0, \"gamma\": 1.2}\n```"},
	"point":              {Signatures: []string{"point(x, y)", "point(x, y, z)"}, Doc: "A point, the same value as `(x, y)` or `(x, y, z)`. Every coordinate must be an integer or a float, and keeps its type."},
	"pop":                {Signatures: []string{"pop(list)"}, Doc: "Removes the last element of `list` and returns it. An error if `list` is empty."},
	"push":               {Signatures: []string{"push(list, x)"}, Doc: "Adds `x` at the end of `list` and returns `list`."},
	"puts":               {Signatures: []string{"puts(a, b, ...)"}, Doc: "Writes its arguments separated by spaces, then a new line. Returns `null`."},
	"range":              {Signatures: []string{"range(end)", "range(start, end)"}, Doc: "`[start, start + 1, ..., end - 1]`, with `start` 0 by default. Empty if `end <= start`."},
	"rest":               {Signatures: []string{"rest(list)"}, Doc: "A new array with every element but the first, or `null` if `list` is empty."},
	"rgb":                {Signatures: []string{"rgb(r, g, b)"}, Doc: "A color from channels. Each is an integer or a float, rounded to the nearest integer (halves away from zero), and must end up from 0 to 255; otherwise it is an error."},
	"rgba":               {Signatures: []string{"rgba(r, g, b, a)"}, Doc: "A color from channels. Each is an integer or a float, rounded to the nearest integer (halves away from zero), and must end up from 0 to 255; otherwise it is an error."},
	"same":               {Signatures: []string{"same(a, b)"}, Doc: "Whether `a` and `b` are the same value: the same array, hash, function, builtin or native, not two with equal contents (`same([1], [1])` is `false`, while `[1] === [1]` is `true`). Numbers, strings, booleans, colors, points and `null` have no identity of their own, so for them it is `===`: `same(1, 1)` is `true`, `same(1, 1.0)` is `false`."},
	"str":                {Signatures: []string{"str(x)"}, Doc: "`x` as a string, the way `puts` shows it."},
	"text.char":          {Signatures: []string{"text.char(code)"}, Doc: "The one-character string with that code. An error for a code outside Unicode or a surrogate."},
	"text.code":          {Signatures: []string{"text.code(s, i)"}, Doc: "The character code of the character at index `i` (negative counts from the end), or `null` if there is none."},
	"text.digest":        {Signatures: []string{"text.digest(s, algorithm = \"sha256\")"}, Doc: "The hash of the text's bytes (UTF-8) as lowercase hex: `\"sha256\"` (64 digits, for telling contents apart) or `\"fnv\"` (FNV-1a of 64 bits, 16 digits: fast, but easy to collide on purpose). The same text gives the same digest on every machine. `text.digest(\"abc\", \"fnv\")` is `\"e71fa2190541574b\"`. Any other algorithm is an error."},
	"text.ends":          {Signatures: []string{"text.ends(s, suffix)"}, Doc: "Booleans."},
	"text.find":          {Signatures: []string{"text.find(s, sub, [from])"}, Doc: "The index of the first `sub` at or after `from` (0 by default; negative counts from the end, clamped), or `-1`. An empty `sub` is found at `from`."},
	"text.join":          {Signatures: []string{"text.join(list, sep)"}, Doc: "The strings of `list` joined with `sep`. Every element must be a string."},
	"text.lines":         {Signatures: []string{"text.lines(s)"}, Doc: "`s` cut at every `\"\\n\"` or `\"\\r\\n\"`. Empty lines stay, and a trailing line break gives a last empty line, so `text.join(text.lines(s), \"\\n\")` gives `s` back (with `\"\\n\"` line breaks)."},
	"text.lower":         {Signatures: []string{"text.lower(s)"}, Doc: "Case changed."},
	"text.number":        {Signatures: []string{"text.number(s)"}, Doc: "The number written in `s` (optional white space, sign, digits, fraction, exponent): an INTEGER when it has no fraction or exponent and fits, else a FLOAT. `null` if `s` isn't a number (`\"12px\"`, `\"\"`, `\"Inf\"`, `\"0x10\"`), never an error for a string."},
	"text.replace":       {Signatures: []string{"text.replace(s, old, new)"}, Doc: "Every `old` replaced by `new`. An empty `old` is an error."},
	"text.slice":         {Signatures: []string{"text.slice(s, start, end)"}, Doc: "The characters from `start` up to `end`. Both are clamped to the string, and a negative one counts from the end (`-1` is the last character's end). `\"\"` if `end` is not after `start`."},
	"text.split":         {Signatures: []string{"text.split(s, sep)"}, Doc: "An array of the pieces between each `sep`. An empty `sep` gives the characters."},
	"text.starts":        {Signatures: []string{"text.starts(s, prefix)"}, Doc: "Booleans."},
	"text.trim":          {Signatures: []string{"text.trim(s)"}, Doc: "Without the white space at both ends."},
	"text.upper":         {Signatures: []string{"text.upper(s)"}, Doc: "Case changed."},
	"time.ms":            {Signatures: []string{"time.ms()"}, Doc: "The milliseconds since the program started, as a float. It only goes forward, even when the system clock changes, so it is the one to measure with.\n```lizard\nlet start = time.ms()\nwork()\nputs(\"took\", time.ms() - start, \"ms\")\n```"},
	"time.now":           {Signatures: []string{"time.now()"}, Doc: "The local date and time as a hash: the integers `year`, `month` (1 to 12), `day`, `hour` (0 to 23), `minute`, `second` and `weekday` (1 for Monday to 7 for Sunday), `epoch`, the seconds since 1970-01-01 UTC as a float with the fractions (the same instant on every machine, to store and subtract), and `utc_offset`, the seconds the local time is ahead of UTC (negative behind it: `-18000` in Bogotá).\n```lizard\nlet now = time.now()\nputs(now.hour, \":\", now.minute)\nlet saved = now.epoch\n-~> later\nputs(int((time.now().epoch - saved) / 3600), \" h ago\")\n```"},
	"time.sleep":         {Signatures: []string{"time.sleep(ms)"}, Doc: "Waits that many milliseconds, an integer or a float from 0 to 3600000 (an hour).\n```lizard\ntime.sleep(500)\n```"},
	"time.utc":           {Signatures: []string{"time.utc()"}, Doc: "`time.now()` in UTC: the same keys, the date and hour of Greenwich, and a `utc_offset` of 0. The `epoch` is the same as `time.now()`'s.\n```lizard\nlet u = time.utc()\nputs(u.year, \"-\", u.month, \"-\", u.day)\n```"},
	"type":               {Signatures: []string{"type(x)"}, Doc: "The name of the type of `x`, like `\"INTEGER\"`."},
	"values":             {Signatures: []string{"values(hash)"}, Doc: "Arrays with the keys or the values, in the order the keys were added."},
	"window.automation":  {Signatures: []string{"window.automation(w, hooks = true)"}, Doc: "Turns automation on (`false` turns it off). `hooks` can instead be a hash of the program's own parts:\n\n| hook | what it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `dump` | a function with no arguments: what it gives is the `app` part of `Dump` (the program's own state) |\n| `stats` | a function with no arguments: what it gives is the `app` part of `Stats` |\n| `commands` | a hash of `name: function(args)`: `Call name args...` calls it with the list of words, numbers as numbers |\n\n```lizard\nwindow.automation(w, {\n  \"dump\": function() { {\"tool\": tool, \"layers\": len(layers)} },\n  \"commands\": {\"Tool\": function(args) { tool = args[0] }},\n})\n```"},
	"window.choose_file": {Signatures: []string{"window.choose_file(w, options = {})"}, Doc: "Opens the system's \"open file\" dialog and returns `null` at once: the window keeps running while the dialog is open. When the user picks files or cancels, the next frame has a `chosen` event with `paths`, a list of absolute paths (empty on cancel). Only one dialog at a time per window; asking for a second is an error.\n\n| Option | Default | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `title` | the system's | the dialog's title or prompt |\n| `types` | any file | extensions to show, like `[\"png\", \"jpg\"]` (a leading dot is fine) |\n| `folder` | the system's | the folder the dialog starts in |\n| `many` | false | the user may pick more than one file |\n\n```lizard\nwindow.choose_file(w, {\"title\": \"Pick an image\", \"types\": [\"png\", \"jpg\"], \"folder\": \"/Users/ana/Pictures\"})\n// then, in tick: if (e.type == \"chosen\" && len(e.paths) > 0) { ... e.paths[0] ... }\n```\nThe dialog is the system's own program, run beside the window: `osascript` on macOS, PowerShell's `OpenFileDialog` on Windows, and `zenity` or else `kdialog` on Linux; with neither installed, `window.choose_file` is an error that says so. If the program fails, the program sees the same as a cancel."},
	"window.dirty":       {Signatures: []string{"window.dirty(target, rect)"}, Doc: "Says which part of the window or of one of its surfaces changed this frame. Without it, a target that was drawn on is shown again whole; with it, only the rectangles declared, each on its own, so a brush dab in one corner and a cursor in the other don't cost the screen between them. Only windows with surfaces compose the frame; a window with none shows its buffer as it is.\n```lizard\nwindow.spans(canvas, dab, ink)\nwindow.dirty(canvas, [x - r, y - r, 2 * r, 2 * r])\n```"},
	"window.inject":      {Signatures: []string{"window.inject(w, event)"}, Doc: "Adds `event`, a hash like the ones `tick` gets, to the next frame, as if the user made it. Keys left out take a plain value: the left button, no `mods`, a mouse's `pen` (a `pen` hash takes `pressure`, `raw`, `source` and `eraser`). A pointer event needs a `pos`, which can be fractional to reach a finer surface's pixels; its `surface` and `surface_pos` are worked out. A `drop` event needs a `pos` and a list of `paths` (strings); a `chosen` event needs only the `paths`. A `resize` event resizes the window like `window.set`.\n```lizard\nlet w = window.open(200, 100, \"\", {\"headless\": true})\nwindow.inject(w, {\"type\": \"down\", \"pos\": (10, 10)})\nwindow.inject(w, {\"type\": \"move\", \"pos\": (50, 10), \"pen\": {\"pressure\": 0.5}})\nwindow.inject(w, {\"type\": \"up\", \"pos\": (50, 10)})\nlet frames = 0\nwindow.run(w, function(events) {\n  handle(events)\n  frames += 1\n  frames < 2\n})\nimg.save(window.screenshot(w), \"after-a-stroke.png\")\n```"},
	"window.needs_paint": {Signatures: []string{"window.needs_paint(w)"}, Doc: "Asks for one more frame: with `on_demand`, `tick` runs next frame even if nothing happens. An animation calls it in every `tick` until it ends."},
	"window.open":        {Signatures: []string{"window.open(w, h, title, options = {})"}, Doc: "Makes a window of `w` by `h` pixels (1 to 8192 each), black at first. It shows on the screen when `window.run` starts, so the program can draw its first picture before. The options are:\n\n| Option | Default | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `scale` | 1 | each pixel shows as `scale` by `scale` on the screen, 1 to 8 |\n| `resizable` | false | the user can resize the window; the buffer follows, and a `resize` event says the new size |\n| `frameless` | false | no title bar or border |\n| `transparent` | false | the pixels the program leaves transparent show what is behind the window (a round splash screen); only `window.open` can choose it, and it stays for the window's life |\n| `floating` | false | the window stays above every other window |\n| `min_size` | none | a point `(w, h)`: the smallest buffer the user can resize the window to |\n| `fps` | 60 | frames a second, 1 to 240 |\n| `hover` | true | `move` events also come with no button down; with false, only while a button is held |\n| `on_demand` | false | `tick` runs only in frames that have events, after `window.needs_paint`, and in the first one: an app that waits for input costs nothing while it waits |\n| `headless` | false | no screen: see Headless windows |\n| `automation` | false | another program can drive the window: see Automation |\n\nAn unknown option is an error, so a typo doesn't go unnoticed.\n```lizard\nlet w = window.open(320, 180, \"game\", {\"scale\": 3, \"fps\": 30})\n```"},
	"window.run":         {Signatures: []string{"window.run(w, tick)"}, Doc: "Shows the window and calls `tick(events)` every frame, with the list of events since the last frame (often empty), until `tick` returns `false` or the window is closed. Closing it sends one last `close` event first. The window shows the buffer again after each frame in which it was drawn on. `tick` can be any function, with default parameters too, and it runs the same on both engines. An error in `tick` closes the window and is `run`'s error, reported like an error in `list.sort`'s comparison: the place of the `window.run` call, then the error inside `tick` (`[line 3, column 11] runtime error: window.run: [line 5, column 9] runtime error: ...`).\n```lizard\nwindow.run(w, function(events) {\n  for (let i = 0; i < len(events); i += 1) {\n    if (events[i].type == \"key\" && events[i].key == \"Escape\") {\n      return false\n    }\n  }\n  draw()\n})\n```"},
	"window.screenshot":  {Signatures: []string{"window.screenshot(w, options = {})"}, Doc: "An image of the window as it shows: its buffer with the surfaces over it, at the finest scale they share (the buffer's own size when it has none, twice it for a window at scale 2 with a surface at scale 1). With `{\"full\": true}` it is every pixel of the screen, the window's size times its scale, surfaces or not: what a user sees, to compare with a picture taken at another scale.\n```lizard\nimg.save(window.screenshot(w), \"shot.png\")\nimg.save(window.screenshot(w, {\"full\": true}), \"screen.png\")\n```"},
	"window.set":         {Signatures: []string{"window.set(w, options)"}, Doc: "Changes the window while it runs, or before: the options of `window.open` but `transparent`, `headless` and `automation`, and these:\n\n| Option | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `size` | a point `(w, h)`: the new size of the buffer, never below `min_size`; the pixels that still fit stay, and a `resize` event comes next frame |\n| `center` | `true` moves the window to the middle of the screen (after the new size) |\n| `title` | the new title |\n\nThe buffer takes a new size or scale at once, so what the program draws next is already at the new size; the window on the screen follows at the end of the frame. This is how a splash screen becomes the app's window:\n```lizard\nwindow.set(w, {\"frameless\": false, \"floating\": false, \"scale\": 2, \"size\": (640, 400),\n  \"min_size\": (320, 200), \"resizable\": true, \"center\": true, \"title\": \"my app\"})\n```"},
	"window.surface":     {Signatures: []string{"window.surface(w, name, rect, scale = 1)"}, Doc: "The window's surface called `name`, shown in `rect`, `[x, y, w, h]` in the window's buffer pixels, each of its pixels `scale` by `scale` screen pixels. Its buffer covers the rect: `window.size(s)` of a surface over `[0, 0, 100, 50]` at scale 1 in a window at scale 2 is `(200, 100)`. The first call makes it, transparent; later calls with the same name move or resize it (keeping the pixels that still fit) and give the same value. An empty rect removes it and gives `null`. Surfaces show in the order they were made, the newest on top; a window can have 64.\n```lizard\nlet w = window.open(640, 400, \"app\", {\"scale\": 2})\nlet canvas = window.surface(w, \"canvas\", [20, 20, 600, 360], 1)\nwindow.clear(canvas, #ffffff)\n```"},
	"window.title":       {Signatures: []string{"window.title(w, text)"}, Doc: "Changes the title."},
}

